---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/PageHeader.astro';
import Tldr from '../../components/Tldr.astro';
import TryItNow from '../../components/TryItNow.astro';
import CopyPrompt from '../../components/CopyPrompt.astro';
import KeyTakeaways from '../../components/KeyTakeaways.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/site';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Choosing Your Model"
  description="Haiku, Sonnet, Opus, and Fable — what each Claude model is best at, what they cost, and how to pick the right brain for the job."
  pageId="models"
  readMinutes={7}
>
  <PageHeader
    icon="🧠"
    title="Choosing Your Model"
    subtitle="Four brains, four price tags. Picking the right one is the single easiest way to get better results for less money."
    readMinutes={7}
  />

  <Tldr>
    <p>
      Claude comes in four models. <strong>Haiku</strong> is fast and nearly free — use it for quick, simple
      tasks. <strong>Sonnet</strong> is the daily driver — it should handle ~90% of your work.
      <strong>Opus</strong> and <strong>Fable</strong> are the expensive heavyweights — reach for them only
      when the problem is genuinely hard. When in doubt: start with Sonnet, escalate only if it struggles.
    </p>
  </Tldr>

  <!-- The lineup -->
  <div class="prose-content">
    <h2>Meet the lineup</h2>
    <p>
      Think of the models like vehicles in a fleet. You don't take the semi truck to grab coffee, and you
      don't haul a battery pack in a hatchback. Same idea here — every model can answer your question, but
      the cost and the depth of thinking vary enormously.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="my-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <!-- Haiku -->
    <div class="rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-5">
      <div class="mb-2 flex items-center justify-between">
        <div class="text-2xl">⚡</div>
        <span class="rounded-full bg-chat-dim px-3 py-1 text-xs font-bold text-chat">$ · cheapest</span>
      </div>
      <h3 class="m-0 mb-1 text-lg font-bold text-text">Haiku</h3>
      <p class="mb-3 text-sm font-medium text-muted">The espresso shot — small, fast, done.</p>
      <p class="m-0 mb-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text">
        <strong>Best at:</strong> quick rewrites, short answers, simple lookups, formatting, repetitive
        tasks you'll run dozens of times.
      </p>
      <ul class="m-0 list-disc pl-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        <li>"Make this email friendlier."</li>
        <li>"What does this acronym mean?"</li>
        <li>"Reformat these 40 part numbers into a table."</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Sonnet -->
    <div class="rounded-[12px] border-2 border-gold/60 bg-gold/5 p-5">
      <div class="mb-2 flex items-center justify-between">
        <div class="text-2xl">🚗</div>
        <span class="rounded-full bg-gold/15 px-3 py-1 text-xs font-bold text-gold">$$ · the default ★</span>
      </div>
      <h3 class="m-0 mb-1 text-lg font-bold text-gold">Sonnet</h3>
      <p class="mb-3 text-sm font-medium text-muted">The daily driver. Start here, always.</p>
      <p class="m-0 mb-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text">
        <strong>Best at:</strong> real everyday work — drafting docs, summarizing meetings, analyzing
        spreadsheets, Co-Work tasks, most coding. The best balance of smarts, speed, and cost.
      </p>
      <ul class="m-0 list-disc pl-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        <li>"Summarize this meeting transcript with action items."</li>
        <li>"Draft a variance analysis from these numbers."</li>
        <li>"Build me a content calendar for Q3."</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Opus -->
    <div class="rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-5">
      <div class="mb-2 flex items-center justify-between">
        <div class="text-2xl">🏋️</div>
        <span class="rounded-full bg-code-dim px-3 py-1 text-xs font-bold text-code">$$$$ · expensive</span>
      </div>
      <h3 class="m-0 mb-1 text-lg font-bold text-text">Opus</h3>
      <p class="mb-3 text-sm font-medium text-muted">The heavyweight. Deep thinking, deep pockets.</p>
      <p class="m-0 mb-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text">
        <strong>Best at:</strong> genuinely hard problems — complex multi-step reasoning, large-context
        analysis, tricky architecture decisions, work where a wrong answer is costly.
      </p>
      <ul class="m-0 list-disc pl-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        <li>"Analyze these 3 supplier contracts and flag conflicting terms."</li>
        <li>"Refactor this module and reason through the edge cases."</li>
        <li>"Evaluate these competing battery sourcing strategies."</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Fable -->
    <div class="rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-5">
      <div class="mb-2 flex items-center justify-between">
        <div class="text-2xl">🧙</div>
        <span class="rounded-full bg-cowork-dim px-3 py-1 text-xs font-bold text-cowork">$$$$$ · premium</span>
      </div>
      <h3 class="m-0 mb-1 text-lg font-bold text-text">Fable</h3>
      <p class="mb-3 text-sm font-medium text-muted">The frontier. Anthropic's most capable model.</p>
      <p class="m-0 mb-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text">
        <strong>Best at:</strong> the hardest problems, long autonomous multi-step work, and — crucially —
        <strong>orchestrating other models</strong> (more on that below). Overkill for everyday tasks.
      </p>
      <ul class="m-0 list-disc pl-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        <li>"Plan and build this entire internal tool end-to-end."</li>
        <li>"Coordinate a team of agents to audit our whole wiki."</li>
        <li>"Untangle this gnarly cross-system data problem."</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Cost intuition -->
  <div class="prose-content">
    <h2>The cost gap is bigger than you think</h2>
    <p>
      This isn't a 10–20% difference. Depending on the pair you compare, the most expensive models cost
      <strong>10–60× more per token</strong> than Haiku. Running your everyday email rewrites through Fable
      is like commissioning an architect to assemble IKEA furniture — they'll do a great job, and you'll
      have paid an absurd amount for an identical bookshelf.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- relative cost bars -->
  <div class="my-6 space-y-3 rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-5">
    <p class="m-0 mb-2 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">Relative cost per task (illustrative)</p>
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
      <span class="w-16 shrink-0 text-sm font-semibold text-text">Haiku</span>
      <div class="h-4 rounded-full bg-chat" style="width: 4%"></div>
      <span class="text-xs text-muted">$</span>
    </div>
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
      <span class="w-16 shrink-0 text-sm font-semibold text-text">Sonnet</span>
      <div class="h-4 rounded-full bg-gold" style="width: 14%"></div>
      <span class="text-xs text-muted">$$</span>
    </div>
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
      <span class="w-16 shrink-0 text-sm font-semibold text-text">Opus</span>
      <div class="h-4 rounded-full bg-code" style="width: 55%"></div>
      <span class="text-xs text-muted">$$$$</span>
    </div>
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
      <span class="w-16 shrink-0 text-sm font-semibold text-text">Fable</span>
      <div class="h-4 rounded-full bg-cowork" style="width: 100%"></div>
      <span class="text-xs text-muted">$$$$$</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <Callout variant="gold" title="The golden rule of model choice">
    <p>
      <strong>Start with Sonnet. Escalate only when it struggles.</strong> If Sonnet's answer is good —
      and it usually is — you just saved real money. If it's clearly out of its depth on a hard problem,
      <em>then</em> re-run with Opus or Fable. Going the other direction (defaulting to the big models
      "just in case") quietly burns budget for zero benefit. Opus and Fable are not "better Sonnet for
      everything" — they're specialists you call in when the job demands it.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <!-- Decision flow -->
  <div class="prose-content">
    <h2>The 10-second decision</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Is it quick and simple?</strong> (rewrite, lookup, reformat) → <strong>Haiku</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Is it normal work?</strong> (draft, summarize, analyze, build) → <strong>Sonnet</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Did Sonnet struggle, or is a wrong answer expensive?</strong> → <strong>Opus</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Is it a huge multi-step project needing a conductor?</strong> → <strong>Fable</strong> (as the orchestrator — see below)</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <!-- Orchestrator pattern -->
  <div class="prose-content">
    <h2>The power move: Fable as the conductor 🎼</h2>
    <p>
      Here's where it gets clever. In Claude Code (and increasingly in Co-Work), the model you're talking to
      can <strong>spawn sub-agents</strong> — separate Claude instances that each handle a piece of the work.
      And each sub-agent can run on a <em>different model</em>.
    </p>
    <p>
      So instead of paying Fable prices for every word of a big project, you use Fable as the
      <strong>orchestrator</strong>: it does the high-level thinking — planning, breaking down the work,
      reviewing results — and delegates the actual labor to cheaper models matched to each task. Haiku
      formats the data. Sonnet writes the pages. Opus handles the one gnarly part. Fable conducts.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fun fact: <strong>this very website was built that way.</strong> One Fable orchestrator planned the
      site and spawned Opus agents for the complex pages, Sonnet agents for the content pages, and Haiku
      agents for the FAQ and glossary — all running in parallel. Premium thinking, economy labor.
    </p>
  </div>

  <TryItNow title="Try the orchestrator pattern">
    <p>
      Paste this into Claude Code on a project — it explicitly asks Fable to conduct and delegate.
      Watch the sub-agents spin up:
    </p>
    <CopyPrompt text="You're running as Fable — act as the orchestrator for this task and spawn sub-agents on the cheapest model that can handle each piece. Task: audit our /docs folder. Spawn Haiku agents to inventory every file and flag anything not touched in 12+ months, Sonnet agents to summarize each stale doc and draft an updated version, and a single Opus agent to review the three most business-critical docs in depth. You handle the plan, coordinate the agents, and give me one consolidated report at the end. Don't do the grunt work yourself — delegate it." />
  </TryItNow>

  <!-- Super-user tips -->
  <div class="prose-content">
    <h2>Becoming a super user: what the pros know</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>You can switch models mid-conversation.</strong> Use the model picker (or
        <code>/model</code> in Claude Code) to drop from Opus back to Sonnet once the hard part is done.
        The conversation context carries over; the price per token drops immediately.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Extended thinking is a dial, not a different model.</strong> Asking Claude to "think
        step by step" or enabling extended thinking makes any model reason more carefully on hard
        problems — often Sonnet-plus-thinking beats Opus-without, at a fraction of the cost.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The model can't fix a vague prompt.</strong> A sharp prompt to Sonnet beats a lazy prompt
        to Fable every single time. If you're not getting good answers, upgrade your
        <a href={href('/prompting/')}>prompt</a> before you upgrade your model.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Fresh chats matter more on big models.</strong> Long conversations re-read everything
        every turn — and on Opus or Fable that re-reading is expensive. Hard problem solved? Start a new
        chat for the next topic.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Every model has a finite context window.</strong> The bigger models give you more room,
        but quality can dip as it fills — and the size varies by model. The
        <a href={href('/context-window/')}>Context Window</a> lesson covers how to watch it and when to compact.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Batch the cheap stuff.</strong> Got 30 product descriptions to clean up? That's one
        Haiku conversation, not 30 Sonnet ones.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>When the stakes are high, get a second opinion.</strong> For an important deliverable,
        have Sonnet draft it, then ask Opus to critique it. Two models for less than the price of
        drafting everything on the big one.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <Callout variant="cowork" title="Where do I actually pick the model?">
    <p>
      In the Claude apps, the model picker is in the chat input area (look for the model name — click it to
      switch). In Claude Code, type <code>/model</code>. If you don't see Opus or Fable as options, that may
      be by design — ask Jake (jake@aptera.us) if your work genuinely needs them.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <KeyTakeaways>
    <ul>
      <li>Start with Sonnet. Drop to Haiku for quick/repetitive tasks. Escalate to Opus or Fable only when the problem genuinely demands it.</li>
      <li>Opus and Fable cost many times more per token — they're specialists, not upgrades for everyday work.</li>
      <li>For big projects, use Fable as an orchestrator that delegates the labor to cheaper sub-agents — premium thinking, economy execution.</li>
    </ul>
  </KeyTakeaways>
</BaseLayout>
